==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|ang|Ēadġȳþ}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*audaz||wealth, riches}} + {{m|gem-pro|*gunþiz||battle}}. Name of a 10th century English saint.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈidɪθ/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-Edith.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-prop}}

# {{given name|en|female|from=Old English}}.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1809|author=[[w:Charles Lamb (writer)|Charles and Mary Lamb]]|title=Poetry for Children: Choosing a Name
|passage='''Edith''''s pretty, but it looks / Better in old English books.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1903|author=Elizabeth Bisland|title=A Candle of Understanding|publisher=Harper&Brothers|page=11
|passage=But suppose she had had an elder sister with a beautiful name like '''Edith'''. My conviction was deep and immovable that had I been named something really pretty like that, I would have found it easy to sew neatly and know my lessons.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=w:Linwood Barclay|title=Fear the Worst|publisher=Banrtam Books|isbn=0553807161|page=76
|passage=- - but they went and gave it to this woman named '''Edith''', if you can believe that any woman with a name like '''Edith''' would have a clue about what's fashionable." "'''Edith''' Head?" I said. "The Oscar-winning costume designer?"}}

